{"qid":"Q752662","names":["Hafsid Dynasty"],"title":"Hafsid Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Hafsids were a Sunni Berber dynasty who governed Ifriqiya (modern Tunisia, northeast Algeria, and Tripolitania) from 1229, founded when Abu Zakariya Yahya broke from the declining Almohad caliphate and built an independent state at Tunis. The dynasty's highest prestige came under Abu Faris Abd al-Aziz II (r. 1394–1434), who reasserted authority over most of the Maghrib and was recognized as caliph by Mamluk Egypt; Tunis in this period was a major commercial hub linking sub-Saharan gold routes with Mediterranean trade. Ottoman naval expeditions captured Tunis in 1534, briefly restored it to the Hafsids in 1535 with Habsburg help, but definitively absorbed the sultanate in 1574 when Khayr al-Din's successor Uluç Ali took the city.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Hafsid territory is well documented through Arabic chronicles and European consular records; interior boundaries with Ziyanid and Marinid rivals shifted frequently and are approximately represented.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Robert Brunschvig, 'La Berbérie orientale sous les Hafsides' (Adrien-Maisonneuve, 1940–47)","url":"https://archive.org/details/laberberieorient0001brun"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Hafsid dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hafsid_dynasty"}],"britannica":"topic/Hafsid-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Hafsid dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Abu Zakariya Yahya I","nativeName":"أبو زكريا يحيى الأول","roman":"Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā I","start":1229,"end":1249,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qi